.\" Generated by scripts/gen_manpage.py from the Typer app (src/hearth/cli.py),
.\" hearth.config.Settings and hearth.status.probes. Do not edit by hand; run:
.\"   uv run \-\-no\-sync python scripts/gen_manpage.py
.TH HEARTH 1 "" "hearth 0.0.1" "HEARTH Manual"
.SH NAME
hearth \- local\-first, no\-egress LLM gateway for Apple Silicon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uv run \-\-no\-sync hearth
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIarguments\fR]
.br
.B hearth
.I command
\fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
HEARTH runs local MLX models (4\-bit Qwen2.5, 3B to 14B) behind an OpenAI\-compatible
HTTP API on 127.0.0.1:8080, so any OpenAI client can use it by changing base_url. It adds
a chat page (/chat), a bounded read\-only tool\-using agent, local RAG, an MCP server so
Claude Code can hand routine work to the local model, a finance pipeline whose arithmetic
is Python Decimal, and LoRA fine\-tuning behind a statistical promotion gate.

Run it from the repository checkout as
.B uv run \-\-no\-sync hearth
.IR command .
A bare
.B uv run
or a partial
.B uv sync
uninstalls mlx, mlx\-lm and mcp, and HEARTH then silently falls back to the echo stub.
Install or repair with one command:
.B uv sync \-\-extra mlx \-\-extra mcp \-\-extra dev \-\-extra files
.SH QUICK START
From the repo root; prefix each hearth command with
.BR "uv run \-\-no\-sync" :
.TP
\fBuv run \-\-no\-sync python \-c "import mlx_lm, mcp, openpyxl, pypdf; print('ok')"\fR
Verify the venv; must print ok.
.TP
\fBhearth doctor \-\-offline\fR
Is it safe to use HEARTH offline right now? Exit 0 = SAFE.
.TP
\fBhearth models list\fR
Which models exist; (default) marks the one that serves.
.TP
\fBhearth run "Say hello in five words."\fR
One local completion.
.TP
\fBhearth serve\fR
API on http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1 and the chat page on /chat.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_FILE_ROOTS=~/notes hearth agent "how many markdown files are there?"\fR
A bounded agent over directories you allow.
.TP
\fBhearth stats\fR
Token savings for this process (zeros in a fresh shell).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "hearth version"
\fBhearth version\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Print the HEARTH version.
.PP
The same version appears in the serve banner and in GET /v1/hearth/admin/health.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth version
.fi
.RE
.SS "hearth doctor"
\fBhearth doctor\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Check the environment; with \-\-offline, decide if HEARTH is safe to use offline.
.PP
Plain doctor checks for Apple Silicon, enough memory, an importable mlx\-lm, and a writable ~/.hearth. A FAIL row is fatal. A WARN row (for example an unregistered HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, which is ignored) is not.
.PP
doctor \-\-offline measures the offline posture of THIS shell's environment instead of reading config: the routing profile the router would load has no remotes; the bind host is loopback; HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS is off; a model that is not on disk fails with no connection attempted; every reachable model is on disk (the row names the path); and train / models convert / models export\-coreml are disk\-only. Run it with the same variables you serve with. It does not inspect firewalls or other processes.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth doctor
hearth doctor \-\-offline; echo $?
HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML=config/routing.finance.yaml hearth doctor \-\-offline
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_HOST, HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS, HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, HEARTH_MODELS_YAML, HEARTH_EMBEDDER, HF_HUB_CACHE, HF_HOME.
.PP
Exit: doctor: 0 ready (warnings allowed), 1 a fatal check failed. doctor \-\-offline: 0 SAFE offline, 1 UNSAFE (the last line names the failed rows).
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Answer 'is it safe to use HEARTH offline right now?' — measures the routing profile, model resolution and every load path; exits 1 when unsafe.
.RE
.SS "hearth models"
\fBhearth models\fR [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
.PP
Model registry: list, download, remove, convert, export to Core ML.
.PP
The registry is config/models.yaml (or HEARTH_MODELS_YAML). 'models pull' is the only command in HEARTH that downloads; every other load reads from disk only.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth models list
hearth models pull mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit
.fi
.RE
.SS "hearth models list"
\fBhearth models list\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
List the models in the registry; (default) marks the one that serves 'auto'.
.PP
The registry is config/models.yaml (or HEARTH_MODELS_YAML). The (default) marker reflects HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL when it names a registered id, so this is the way to confirm which model will answer. Listing does not check that weights are on disk; hearth doctor \-\-offline does.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth models list
HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL=mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit \e
  hearth models list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_MODELS_YAML, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL.
.SS "hearth models pull"
\fBhearth models pull\fR [OPTIONS] MODEL_ID
.PP
Download a registry model's weights into ~/.hearth/models (needs the network).
.PP
The ONE deliberate download path in HEARTH: serve, chat, agent, MCP, RAG, train, models convert and models export\-coreml all load from disk only and fail rather than fetch. Only registry ids are accepted (hearth models list). Respects the HF_ENDPOINT mirror and HF_HUB_OFFLINE; no host is hardcoded.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth models pull mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit
HF_ENDPOINT=https://mirror.example \e
  hearth models pull mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-14B\-Instruct\-4bit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_MODELS_YAML, HF_ENDPOINT, HF_HUB_OFFLINE.
.PP
Exit: 0 downloaded (or nothing to pull, e.g. echo); 1 unknown model id.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIMODEL_ID\fR
Registry model id to download. (required)
.RE
.SS "hearth models rm"
\fBhearth models rm\fR [OPTIONS] MODEL_ID
.PP
Delete a model's downloaded copy from ~/.hearth/models.
.PP
Removes only HEARTH's own copy (models\-\-ORG\-\-NAME). It does not touch the Hugging Face hub cache (HF_HUB_CACHE / ~/.cache/huggingface/hub), which the loader also reads, so a model can still resolve after rm; hearth doctor \-\-offline shows where.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth models rm mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_MODELS_YAML.
.PP
Exit: 0 removed; 1 unknown id, or not present under ~/.hearth/models.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIMODEL_ID\fR
Registry model id to remove. (required)
.RE
.SS "hearth models convert"
\fBhearth models convert\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Quantize or convert a checkpoint on disk into an MLX\-servable model.
.PP
Needs the mlx extra and the source on disk (hearth models pull, or a local path). It never downloads unless HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS=1. To serve the result, add it to config/models.yaml (the registry is data).
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth models convert \-\-source ./my\-checkpoint \e
  \-\-out ~/.hearth/models/mine\-q8 \-\-q\-bits 8
hearth models convert \-\-source ./my\-checkpoint \e
  \-\-out ./my\-checkpoint\-mlx \-\-no\-quantize
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS, HF_HUB_CACHE, HF_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 converted; 1 invalid options, source not on disk, output exists, or mlx missing.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-source\fR \fITEXT\fR
Source checkpoint: HF repo id or local path to convert. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
Output dir for the MLX\-format model. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-quantize\fR / \fB\-\-no\-quantize\fR
Quantize the model (else format\-convert only). (default: \-\-quantize)
.TP
\fB\-\-q\-bits\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Quantization bit width (2/3/4/6/8). (default: 4)
.TP
\fB\-\-q\-group\-size\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Quantization group size. (default: 64)
.RE
.SS "hearth models export\-coreml"
\fBhearth models export\-coreml\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Export a checkpoint on disk to a Core ML .mlpackage for the offline Swift path.
.PP
The .mlpackage is loaded by the Swift CoreMLProvider (swift/OFFLINE.md) for fully\-offline, daemon\-free inference. Needs the coreml extra (add \-\-extra coreml to the one\-command sync) and the source on disk; it never downloads unless HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS=1.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth models export\-coreml \-\-source ./my\-checkpoint \e
  \-\-out ~/.hearth/coreml/mine
hearth models export\-coreml \-\-source ./ckpt \-\-out ./ckpt.mlpackage \e
  \-\-compute\-units cpuOnly
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS, HF_HUB_CACHE, HF_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 exported; 1 invalid options, source not on disk, or coremltools missing.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-source\fR \fITEXT\fR
Source checkpoint: HF repo id or local path to export. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
Output .mlpackage dir for the Core ML model. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-compute\-units\fR \fITEXT\fR
Runtime placement: all / cpuAndNeuralEngine / cpuAndGPU / cpuOnly. (default: cpuAndNeuralEngine)
.TP
\fB\-\-precision\fR \fITEXT\fR
Weight precision: float16 / float32 / int8. (default: float16)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-seq\-len\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Fixed sequence length to trace/export at (Core ML is static). (default: 512)
.TP
\fB\-\-stateful\fR / \fB\-\-no\-stateful\fR
Export the stateful KV\-cache model (Approach B, O(1)/token, CPU\-only, Qwen2; ADR\-011). Default off keeps Approach A (padded prefill, ANE) as the shipped path. (default: \-\-no\-stateful)
.RE
.SS "hearth serve"
\fBhearth serve\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Start the OpenAI\-compatible gateway and the /chat page on 127.0.0.1:8080.
.PP
Serves /v1/chat/completions, /v1/models, /v1/embeddings and the /v1/hearth/* extensions to any OpenAI client (base_url http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1). For the built\-in chat page open http://127.0.0.1:8080/chat and paste the token once.
.PP
Routing profile: config/routing.yaml unless HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML names another. The default defines zero remote models, so no request can leave this machine. A relative HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML resolves against the repo root, ~ is expanded, and a named file that does not exist stops serve from starting.
.PP
Token: every /v1/* route needs 'Authorization: Bearer TOKEN'. The token is created on first run in ~/.hearth/token (mode 0600; $HEARTH_HOME/token). /chat, /v1/hearth/admin/health and /v1/hearth/admin/ready need no token.
.PP
Readiness: on the mlx backend the default model loads in the background at startup (HEARTH_WARMUP). GET /v1/hearth/admin/ready answers 200 once the default has loaded (or, with HEARTH_WARMUP=false, once its weights are on disk) and stays 200 if it is later evicted to make room; it answers 503 'loading' during the first load, 'failed' with a reason when the load failed or the weights are missing, and 'stub' when the echo fallback is answering. Check backend= in the banner: 'echo' is a test stub.
.PP
Settings are read once at startup, so restart after changing any HEARTH_* variable.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth serve
hearth serve \-\-port 8081
HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML=config/routing.finance.yaml hearth serve
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOST, HEARTH_PORT, HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, HEARTH_MODELS_YAML, HEARTH_REQUIRE_AUTH, HEARTH_WARMUP, HEARTH_RAM_CEILING_GB, HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS, HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: runs until interrupted (Ctrl\-C). Exits non\-zero without serving when the selected routing profile does not exist.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-host\fR \fITEXT\fR
Bind host (default from HEARTH_HOST / 127.0.0.1). Anything but loopback exposes the API to the network and makes doctor \-\-offline UNSAFE.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Bind port (default from HEARTH_PORT / 8080).
.RE
.SS "hearth run"
\fBhearth run\fR [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]
.PP
Run one prompt through the local model and print the answer.
.PP
Always local: run never escalates, whatever the routing profile. It has no tools, so it cannot open a file you mention: pass \-\-file to send a file's text as the prompt, or use hearth agent. The answer goes to stdout; a 'served by MODEL via BACKEND' line goes to stderr, so piping the answer stays clean.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth run "Summarize: HEARTH keeps inference on this Mac."
hearth run \-\-file notes.txt \-\-max\-tokens 256
echo "crash when saving" | hearth run \-\-intent classify
hearth run \-\-model mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit "hello"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, HEARTH_MODELS_YAML, HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML, HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 answered; 1 empty prompt; 2 \-\-model names no servable registry model, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL names an unregistered id, or the selected routing profile is missing.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIPROMPT\fR
Prompt text. Omit to read from stdin. (optional)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-tokens\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Max tokens to generate. (default: 512)
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read the prompt from this file instead of the argument.
.TP
\fB\-\-intent\fR \fITEXT\fR
Task class hint that skips classification: summarize, extract, classify, rank, draft, code, reason or chat. Picks that class's model under 'auto'.
.TP
\fB\-\-model\fR \fITEXT\fR
Registry model id to serve this prompt. 'auto' lets the routing ladder pick. (default: auto)
.RE
.SS "hearth agent"
\fBhearth agent\fR [OPTIONS] [TASK]
.PP
Run a bounded, read\-only, tool\-using local agent over your own files.
.PP
The agent plans, calls one tool, reads the result, and repeats until it answers or hits a bound. Every generation is local and it never escalates. Unlike hearth run (and plain /v1/chat/completions) it can actually read the files it talks about, so a question about a directory is answered from the filesystem, not from imagination.
.PP
Tools are assembled from what is present, and the header line lists them: list_files and read_file always (deny\-by\-default: they refuse every path outside HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS); rag_search with \-\-collection; finance_total / finance_explain / finance_rows when a ledger exists at ~/.hearth/finance/ledger.db. There are no write, shell or network tools, and deliberately no flag to add or un\-vet tools: only tools whose code lives in hearth.agent run, which is what the no\-network test covers. See docs/AGENT.md.
.PP
Read the step table before trusting the answer: small local models retry failed ideas and invent plausible filenames.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS=~/statements hearth agent "how many CSV files are there?"
hearth agent \-\-collection notes "when should I descale the kettle?"
HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS=~/notes hearth agent \-\-json "list the md files" > run.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS (colon\-separated readable directories; unset = none), HEARTH_FILE_MAX_BYTES, HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML, HEARTH_EMBEDDER, HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit (the stop reason): 0 the model answered, and only then; 1 the run stopped at a bound or a failure (max_iterations, timeout, token_budget, invalid_output, provider_error, egress_refused) and there is no answer; 2 never started (an impossible bound, an empty \-\-collection, an unknown \-\-model, or nothing reachable).
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fITASK\fR
What the agent should do. Omit to read from stdin. (optional)
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\fR \fITEXT\fR
Offer rag_search, pinned to this indexed RAG collection.
.TP
\fB\-\-finance\fR / \fB\-\-no\-finance\fR
Offer the read\-only ledger tools when a finance ledger exists. (default: \-\-finance)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-iterations\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Hard cap on model turns; hitting it stops the run. (default: 8)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-seconds\fR \fIFLOAT\fR
Hard wall\-clock cap in seconds; hitting it stops the run. (default: 180.0)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-tokens\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Hard cap on prompt+completion tokens across every step of the run. (default: 24000)
.TP
\fB\-\-steps\fR / \fB\-\-no\-steps\fR
Print the step\-by\-step transcript before the answer. (default: \-\-steps)
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Print the loop's own full transcript (raw model output per step).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the whole run as JSON instead of prose (for scripting).
.TP
\fB\-\-model\fR \fITEXT\fR
Registry model id for every step. 'auto' lets the routing ladder pick. (default: auto)
.RE
.SS "hearth mcp"
\fBhearth mcp\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Start the stdio MCP server so Claude Code can hand subtasks to the local model.
.PP
Exposes hearth_summarize, hearth_classify, hearth_extract, hearth_draft, the path\-taking hearth_summarize_file / hearth_classify_file / hearth_extract_file, and hearth_rag_query. Every tool runs on the local model with escalation disabled, under any routing profile, in\-process (no HTTP, no token). Prefer the *_file tools for anything confidential: HEARTH opens the file, so its content never enters the calling agent's context. Those tools refuse every path outside HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS.
.PP
Register it with the venv's absolute path; an MCP subprocess has no activated venv and no guaranteed working directory. Needs the mcp extra.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth mcp
claude mcp add hearth \-e HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS="$HOME/docs" \-\- \e
  ~/Claude/apps/HEARTH/.venv/bin/hearth mcp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS, HEARTH_FILE_MAX_BYTES, HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML, HEARTH_EMBEDDER, HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 when the client closes stdin; 1 when the mcp extra is not installed.
.SS "hearth stats"
\fBhearth stats\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Show token\-savings, escalation and latency rollups for THIS process.
.PP
Metrics are kept in memory per process and hold no prompt or response text. A fresh 'hearth stats' therefore always shows zeros; the numbers accumulate inside a running hearth serve. For a running server, read GET /v1/hearth/admin/metrics?since=24h (same rollup, as JSON, token required).
.PP
A request that ended in an error is counted in requests and in failed / failure rate; backend mix and latency count answered requests only. Escalations failed counts every request whose remote call errored, whether the local fallback then answered or failed too: the remote may have received the prompt either way.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth stats
hearth stats \-\-since 24h
curl \-s \-H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat ~/.hearth/token)" \e
  "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/hearth/admin/metrics?since=24h"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fITEXT\fR
Rollup window, e.g. 7d / 24h / 30m (default: all).
.RE
.SS "hearth rag"
\fBhearth rag\fR [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
.PP
Local RAG: chunk and index files into a collection, then search it.
.PP
Collections live in ~/.hearth/rag/NAME.db and hold the raw chunk text. The default embedder (HEARTH_EMBEDDER=hash) is lexical: queries must share words with the text.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth rag ingest ~/notes \-\-collection notes
hearth rag query "descale the kettle" \-\-collection notes
.fi
.RE
.SS "hearth rag ingest"
\fBhearth rag ingest\fR [OPTIONS] PATH
.PP
Chunk, embed and store a file or directory into a local RAG collection.
.PP
The collection is ~/.hearth/rag/COLLECTION.db and holds the raw chunk text; delete that file to purge it. The default embedder (HEARTH_EMBEDDER=hash) is offline and lexical, not semantic. HEARTH_EMBEDDER=mlx does not work today (docs/BUGS.md B\-011).
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth rag ingest ~/notes \-\-collection notes
hearth rag ingest README.md \-\-collection docs \-\-size 400 \-\-overlap 50
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_EMBEDDER, HEARTH_EMBED_DIM, HEARTH_EMBED_MODEL, HEARTH_VECTOR_STORE, HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIPATH\fR
File or directory to ingest. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\fR \fITEXT\fR
Target collection name. (default: default)
.TP
\fB\-\-size\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Chunk size in characters. (default: 800)
.TP
\fB\-\-overlap\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Chunk overlap in characters. (default: 100)
.RE
.SS "hearth rag query"
\fBhearth rag query\fR [OPTIONS] QUERY
.PP
Search a RAG collection; with \-\-answer, have the local model answer from the hits.
.PP
Prints a table of score, source and text for the top \-\-k chunks. \-\-answer sends the retrieved chunks plus your question to the local model (never escalated).
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth rag query "how often to descale the kettle" \-\-collection notes \-\-k 2
hearth rag query "descale" \-\-collection notes \-\-answer
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_EMBEDDER, HEARTH_EMBED_DIM, HEARTH_VECTOR_STORE, HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0, including for a missing or empty collection (it says so).
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIQUERY\fR
Query text. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\fR \fITEXT\fR
Collection to search. (default: default)
.TP
\fB\-\-k\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Number of chunks to retrieve. (default: 6)
.TP
\fB\-\-answer\fR
Answer with the local model grounded in retrieved chunks.
.RE
.SS "hearth train"
\fBhearth train\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Train a LoRA adapter on disk\-resident weights and register it as a candidate.
.PP
Needs the mlx extra and the base model on disk (hearth models pull). It never downloads unless HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS=1, and it checks the base model before creating the run directory, so a failed run leaves nothing behind. Training only produces a candidate; it serves nothing until it passes the promotion gate: hearth prereg init, commit the file, then hearth eval ADAPTER \-\-prereg FILE \-\-promote.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth train \-\-task classify \-\-base mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit \e
  \-\-data data.jsonl
hearth train \-\-task classify \-\-base mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit \e
  \-\-data data.jsonl \-\-iters 50 \-\-no\-register
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS, HF_HUB_CACHE, HF_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 trained (and registered); 1 dataset error, base model not on disk, mlx missing, or the training process failed (its exit code and stderr tail are shown).
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-task\fR \fITEXT\fR
Task class the adapter targets (e.g. extract). (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-base\fR \fITEXT\fR
Base model registry id to fine\-tune (LoRA); must be on disk. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-data\fR \fIPATH\fR
Dataset JSONL: a hearth.dataset.header line, then at least 2 {"prompt","completion"} or {"messages"} rows. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
Output dir for the run (default: ~/.hearth/train/<run\-id>).
.TP
\fB\-\-iters\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Training iterations. (default: 200)
.TP
\fB\-\-register\fR / \fB\-\-no\-register\fR
Register the result as a candidate adapter. (default: \-\-register)
.RE
.SS "hearth eval"
\fBhearth eval\fR [OPTIONS] ADAPTER_ID
.PP
Score a candidate adapter against a golden set, and optionally promote it.
.PP
The candidate is scored with an objective metric at temperature 0 and compared, pairwise per example, against the incumbent: the promoted adapter for the task, or the base model when none is promoted. To pass, it must clear the significance level (exact McNemar or paired bootstrap), exceed the margin, have at least min_n examples, and beat the empty / majority\-label / copy\-input baselines. \-\-promote additionally requires a \-\-prereg that is git\-committed, unmodified and matches this run; its bar overrides \-\-alpha / \-\-margin / \-\-min\-n. Real scores need HEARTH_BACKEND=mlx; the echo backend runs the plumbing only.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth eval ADAPTER_ID \-\-golden golden.jsonl
hearth eval ADAPTER_ID \-\-golden golden.jsonl \-\-prereg prereg/c.yaml \e
  \-\-report\-json r.json
hearth eval ADAPTER_ID \-\-golden golden.jsonl \-\-prereg prereg/c.yaml \e
  \-\-promote
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_HOME, HEARTH_MODELS_YAML.
.PP
Exit: without \-\-promote, 0 once the gate was measured, whether it passed or failed (read the gate: line); 1 when it refused to measure (unknown adapter, bad golden set or prereg, temperature above 0, non\-determinism). With \-\-promote, 0 only when the adapter was promoted, else 1.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIADAPTER_ID\fR
Candidate adapter id to score. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-golden\fR \fIPATH\fR
Golden set JSONL ({"prompt","expected"} per line). (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-metric\fR \fITEXT\fR
Objective metric: 'f1' or 'exact'. (default: f1)
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR \fITEXT\fR
Optional system prompt sent with every example.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-tokens\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Max tokens per generation. (default: 64)
.TP
\fB\-\-temperature\fR \fIFLOAT\fR
Decode temperature. 0.0 (greedy): a re\-rollable score is not a measurement. (default: 0.0)
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-sampling\fR
Permit \-\-temperature > 0 (scores become re\-rollable).
.TP
\fB\-\-base\fR \fITEXT\fR
Base model id (default: the adapter's recorded base_model).
.TP
\fB\-\-alpha\fR \fIFLOAT\fR
Significance level (a \-\-prereg overrides it). (default: 0.05)
.TP
\fB\-\-margin\fR \fIFLOAT\fR
Minimum effect the candidate must exceed (a \-\-prereg overrides it). (default: 0.0)
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-n\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Minimum golden\-set size the gate licenses (\-\-prereg overrides it). (default: 30)
.TP
\fB\-\-prereg\fR \fIPATH\fR
Pre\-registration YAML: required by \-\-promote, and its bar wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-report\-json\fR \fIPATH\fR
Write the reports + gate result here (feeds promote).
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-determinism\fR
Re\-generate a few golden prompts and refuse if the answers differ.
.TP
\fB\-\-promote\fR
Promote the candidate if the gate passes (needs \-\-prereg).
.RE
.SS "hearth adapters"
\fBhearth adapters\fR [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
.PP
LoRA adapter registry: list, promote (from a measured report), retire.
.PP
Adapters are recorded in ~/.hearth/adapters.json as candidate, promoted or retired. A promoted adapter is applied automatically to its task class when its base serves.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth adapters list \-\-status candidate
hearth adapters retire ADAPTER_ID
.fi
.RE
.SS "hearth adapters list"
\fBhearth adapters list\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
List registered adapters with their task, base model, status and eval scores.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth adapters list
hearth adapters list \-\-task classify \-\-status promoted
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0; 1 an invalid \-\-status.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-task\fR \fITEXT\fR
Filter by task class.
.TP
\fB\-\-status\fR \fITEXT\fR
Filter by status: candidate, promoted or retired.
.RE
.SS "hearth adapters promote"
\fBhearth adapters promote\fR [OPTIONS] ADAPTER_ID
.PP
Promote a candidate from a measured eval report and a committed pre\-registration.
.PP
The gate is recomputed here from the report's per\-example vectors under the bar in the committed pre\-registration: the report is evidence, not a verdict. Both \-\-report (from hearth eval \-\-report\-json) and \-\-prereg are required. The usual one\-step path is hearth eval ADAPTER \-\-golden G \-\-prereg P \-\-promote. The old \-\-candidate\-score / \-\-incumbent\-score flags are removed: a typed score names no golden set, metric or model, so it is not evidence.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth adapters promote ADAPTER_ID \-\-report r.json \e
  \-\-prereg prereg/classify.yaml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 promoted; 1 refused (missing or unusable report, uncommitted or mismatched prereg, gate failed); 2 the removed typed\-score flags were used.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIADAPTER_ID\fR
Adapter id to promote. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-report\fR \fIPATH\fR
Eval report JSON written by `hearth eval \-\-report\-json`.
.TP
\fB\-\-prereg\fR \fIPATH\fR
Committed pre\-registration YAML declaring the bar.
.RE
.SS "hearth adapters retire"
\fBhearth adapters retire\fR [OPTIONS] ADAPTER_ID
.PP
Retire an adapter so it is no longer served, even if it was promoted.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth adapters retire ADAPTER_ID
.fi
.RE
.PP
Env: HEARTH_HOME.
.PP
Exit: 0 retired; 1 unknown adapter.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIADAPTER_ID\fR
Adapter id to retire. (required)
.RE
.SS "hearth prereg"
\fBhearth prereg\fR [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
.PP
Pre\-registration: declare the eval bar before training, then commit it.
.PP
hearth eval \-\-promote refuses unless a committed, unmodified pre\-registration declares the bar the run is judged against.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth prereg init \-\-task classify \-\-golden golden.jsonl \e
  \-\-out prereg/classify.yaml
hearth prereg check prereg/classify.yaml \-\-golden golden.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.SS "hearth prereg init"
\fBhearth prereg init\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
Scaffold a pre\-registration for a golden set; then write the prose and commit it.
.PP
The file pins the golden set by content sha and the decode parameters by fingerprint, so the gate can later prove the run it judged is the run that was registered. hypothesis, stopping_rule and kill_condition are left blank on purpose: prereg check and eval \-\-promote refuse the file until you write them, because a bar written by the tool is not a pre\-registration.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth prereg init \-\-task classify \-\-golden golden.jsonl
hearth prereg init \-\-task classify \-\-golden golden.jsonl \e
  \-\-out prereg/classify.yaml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit: 0 written (or printed); 1 the golden set is unreadable or empty, or the bar is looser than the gate allows (alpha above 0.05, negative min\-effect, min\-n below 30).
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fB\-\-task\fR \fITEXT\fR
Task class the adapter serves. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-golden\fR \fIPATH\fR
Golden set JSONL to pin by content sha. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
Write here instead of printing to stdout.
.TP
\fB\-\-metric\fR \fITEXT\fR
Objective metric: 'exact' or 'f1'. (default: exact)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-tokens\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Registered generation max tokens. (default: 64)
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR \fITEXT\fR
System prompt (hashed into the config).
.TP
\fB\-\-alpha\fR \fIFLOAT\fR
Registered significance level. (default: 0.05)
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-effect\fR \fIFLOAT\fR
Minimum lift that would count. (default: 0.0)
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-n\fR \fIINTEGER\fR
Minimum golden\-set size. (default: 30)
.RE
.SS "hearth prereg check"
\fBhearth prereg check\fR [OPTIONS] PATH
.PP
Validate a pre\-registration and report whether git has it committed, unmodified.
.PP
Prints the registered bar (task, metric, golden sha, alpha, min_effect, min_n, test, baselines). Refuses a file whose hypothesis / stopping_rule / kill_condition are blank or that drops a default baseline. With \-\-golden, also checks the golden set still hashes to the registered sha.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth prereg check prereg/classify.yaml
hearth prereg check prereg/classify.yaml \-\-golden golden.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit: 0 valid, committed and unmodified (and the golden set matches); 1 otherwise.
.PP
Arguments and options:
.RS 4
.TP
\fIPATH\fR
Pre\-registration YAML to validate. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-golden\fR \fIPATH\fR
Also check this golden set still hashes to the registered sha.
.RE
.SS "hearth plugins"
\fBhearth plugins\fR [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
.PP
Third\-party providers, vector stores and embedders found via entry points.
.PP
See docs/PLUGINS.md to write one.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth plugins list
.fi
.RE
.SS "hearth plugins list"
\fBhearth plugins list\fR [OPTIONS]
.PP
List plugins registered under HEARTH's entry\-point groups, and whether each loaded.
.PP
Covers the hearth.providers, hearth.vector_stores and hearth.embedders groups. Status is 'ok' when the plugin imported and satisfied its Protocol, else why it was skipped; a broken plugin is reported here instead of crashing the server. Select one by name with HEARTH_BACKEND, HEARTH_VECTOR_STORE or HEARTH_EMBEDDER. See docs/PLUGINS.md.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth plugins list
.fi
.RE
.SH PRIVACY AND OFFLINE USE
The default routing profile (config/routing.yaml) defines zero remote models: every task
class runs locally, so the router has nowhere to send a prompt. Escalation to a frontier
model exists only under the opt\-in config/routing.remote.yaml. hearth run, hearth agent
and every MCP tool never escalate, under any profile.

Model loads are disk\-only. serve, chat, agent, MCP, RAG, train, models convert and models
export\-coreml resolve weights from $HEARTH_HOME/models, then the Hugging Face hub cache,
and fail with ModelNotOnDiskError instead of downloading. hearth models pull is the only
command that downloads.

The gateway binds to loopback and requires a bearer token. Metrics hold token counts and
metadata, never prompt or response text, in memory only.

.B hearth doctor \-\-offline
measures this posture for the current shell's environment and exits 1 when it is unsafe.
It does not cover the calling agent (a cloud agent that read a file has already sent it;
hand HEARTH a path via the *_file MCP tools instead), machine\-level containment
(firewalls, other processes), or data at rest (RAG collections, training runs and the
ledger hold real copies of your text). See docs/PRIVACY.md.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Settings are read from HEARTH_* variables (env_prefix HEARTH_, extra ignored): a misspelled name is silently ignored; scripts/hearth_status.py \-\-section environment flags it. hearth serve and hearth mcp read settings once at startup. Booleans accept 1/0/true/false/yes/no.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_HOST\fR (default: 127.0.0.1)
Bind address for hearth serve. Anything but loopback (127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost) exposes the API to the network and makes doctor \-\-offline UNSAFE. \-\-host overrides it.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_PORT\fR (default: 8080)
Bind port for hearth serve. \-\-port overrides it.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_BACKEND\fR (default: auto)
Inference backend: auto (mlx when mlx\-lm imports, otherwise the echo stub, silently), mlx (fail loudly if MLX is missing), echo (deterministic test stub, no weights), or a plugin name. Check backend= in the serve banner.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_REQUIRE_AUTH\fR (default: true)
Require 'Authorization: Bearer TOKEN' on every /v1/* route. false is for throwaway local testing only.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_EMBEDDER\fR (default: hash)
RAG embedder: hash (offline, lexical; the default), mlx (does not work today, docs/BUGS.md B\-011), or a plugin name.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_EMBED_DIM\fR (default: 256)
Vector dimension of the hash embedder.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_EMBED_MODEL\fR (default: mlx\-community/bge\-small\-en\-v1.5\-mlx)
Model id of the mlx embedder (only used when HEARTH_EMBEDDER=mlx).
.TP
\fBHEARTH_VECTOR_STORE\fR (default: sqlite)
RAG vector store: sqlite (default), sqlite\-vec (needs the vec extra), or a plugin name.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_RAM_CEILING_GB\fR (default: 24.0)
RAM budget (GB, registry estimates) for resident models. Loading a model that would exceed it evicts the least\-recently\-used one. The real GPU working\-set ceiling of a 36 GB M3 Pro is about 30 GB; scripts/hearth_status.py measures it.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_WARMUP\fR (default: true)
Load the default model in the background when hearth serve starts, so the first request is warm and /v1/hearth/admin/ready can turn 200. No effect on echo.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_FILE_ROOTS\fR (default: "" (empty))
Colon\-separated directories the agent's read_file / list_files and the MCP *_file tools may read under. Deny\-by\-default: empty refuses every read; there is no implicit root. ~ is expanded; entries that are not existing directories are dropped.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_FILE_MAX_BYTES\fR (default: 2000000)
Largest single file those tools will read; bigger files are refused, not truncated.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_ALLOW_DOWNLOADS\fR (default: false)
Let a model load fetch weights that are not on disk. Leave it off: with it on, doctor \-\-offline reports UNSAFE. hearth models pull downloads regardless.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_HOME\fR (default: ~/.hearth)
Root of HEARTH's state: token, models/, rag/, adapters.json, train/, finance/ledger.db, handoff/. Point it at a scratch directory for experiments.
.PP
Read directly from the environment, outside Settings:
.TP
\fBHEARTH_BASE_MODEL\fR (default: mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-Coder\-7B\-Instruct\-4bit)
Base model for scripts/train_lora_real.sh only.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_CANDIDATE_SCORE\fR (default: unset)
scripts/train_lora_real.sh only; feeds the removed typed\-score promote path.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL\fR (default: the default: key of config/models.yaml)
Registry id served when a request names no model (or 'auto' finds no per\-class rung). Must be a registered id: an unregistered value is currently ignored with a warning, doctor shows a WARN row, and /v1/hearth/admin/ready reports failed (docs/BUGS.md B\-047 tracks making it a startup error). The name is HEARTH_DEFAULT_MODEL, not HEARTH_MODEL; misspelled HEARTH_* names are silently ignored. Confirm with hearth models list.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_INCUMBENT_SCORE\fR (default: unset)
scripts/train_lora_real.sh only; feeds the removed typed\-score promote path.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_MODELS_YAML\fR (default: <repo>/config/models.yaml)
Alternate model registry file.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_ROUTING_YAML\fR (default: <repo>/config/routing.yaml)
Routing profile: which task classes run locally and which may escalate. ~ is expanded and a relative path resolves against the repo root, not the current directory. A named file that does not exist is an error (serve, run and agent refuse to start; doctor \-\-offline fails). The shipped profiles routing.yaml, routing.private.yaml and routing.finance.yaml define zero remotes.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_TRAIN_DATA\fR (default: unset)
Dataset for scripts/train_lora_real.sh only.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_TRAIN_ITERS\fR (default: 200)
Iterations for scripts/train_lora_real.sh only.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_TRAIN_OUT\fR (default: unset)
Output directory for scripts/train_lora_real.sh only.
.TP
\fBHEARTH_TRAIN_TASK\fR (default: extract)
Task class for scripts/train_lora_real.sh only.
.PP
Not HEARTH's, but they matter:
.TP
\fBHF_HUB_CACHE, HF_HOME\fR
Where the model resolver's Hugging Face cache lookup looks (HF_HUB_CACHE, else HF_HOME/hub, else ~/.cache/huggingface/hub), after $HEARTH_HOME/models. Do not export them globally to paper over a split.
.TP
\fBHF_ENDPOINT\fR
Mirror used by hearth models pull.
.TP
\fBHF_HUB_OFFLINE\fR
Not needed by HEARTH's own load paths, which are disk\-only already; scripts/hearth_private.sh sets it for any other hub call.
.TP
\fBANTHROPIC_API_KEY\fR
Only for the opt\-in escalation profile config/routing.remote.yaml (needs the remote extra).
.SH FILES
Everything under ~/.hearth moves with HEARTH_HOME. config/ paths are relative to the repo root.
.TP
\fI~/.hearth/token\fR
Bearer token, mode 0600, created on first serve.
.TP
\fI~/.hearth/models/\fR
Weights downloaded by hearth models pull (hub layout).
.TP
\fI~/.hearth/rag/COLLECTION.db\fR
A RAG collection, including the raw chunk text.
.TP
\fI~/.hearth/adapters.json\fR
The adapter registry (candidate / promoted / retired).
.TP
\fI~/.hearth/train/RUN_ID/\fR
Training runs and their adapters.
.TP
\fI~/.hearth/finance/ledger.db\fR
The finance ledger; its presence enables the agent's finance tools.
.TP
\fIconfig/models.yaml\fR
The model registry (override: HEARTH_MODELS_YAML).
.TP
\fIconfig/routing*.yaml\fR
Routing profiles (select: HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML).
.TP
\fIman/hearth.1\fR
This page, generated by scripts/gen_manpage.py.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success. For hearth agent: the model answered. For hearth doctor \-\-offline: SAFE. For hearth eval without \-\-promote: the gate was measured (read its PASS/FAIL line).
.TP
\fB1\fR
Failure or refusal: a fatal doctor check, UNSAFE offline, an empty prompt, an agent run that stopped at a bound, a refused promotion, a model not on disk, an invalid option value.
.TP
\fB2\fR
Usage error (unknown option or command), or the work never started: an unknown \-\-model for run or agent, an agent with nothing it can reach, the removed \-\-candidate\-score flags.
.PP
Each command's own exit codes are listed under COMMANDS.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check offline safety and see which file each model resolves from:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth doctor \-\-offline; echo $?
.fi
.RE
.PP
Serve the two\-tier local finance ladder (3B for classify, 14B for summarize):
.RS 4
.nf
HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML=config/routing.finance.yaml hearth doctor \-\-offline
HEARTH_ROUTING_YAML=config/routing.finance.yaml hearth serve
.fi
.RE
.PP
Call the API with curl:
.RS 4
.nf
export HEARTH_TOKEN="$(cat ~/.hearth/token)"
curl \-s http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/chat/completions \e
  \-H "Authorization: Bearer $HEARTH_TOKEN" \-H "Content\-Type: application/json" \e
  \-d '{"model":"auto","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hello"}]}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pick a model per request, and see what is loaded:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth run \-\-model mlx\-community/Qwen2.5\-3B\-Instruct\-4bit "hello"
curl \-s \-H "Authorization: Bearer $HEARTH_TOKEN" \e
  http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/hearth/admin/models
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask the agent about a directory, with the run as JSON:
.RS 4
.nf
HEARTH_FILE_ROOTS=~/notes hearth agent \-\-json "how many CSV files are there?"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Index notes and query them:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth rag ingest ~/notes \-\-collection notes
hearth rag query "descale the kettle" \-\-collection notes \-\-answer
.fi
.RE
.PP
The promotion path for a trained adapter:
.RS 4
.nf
hearth prereg init \-\-task classify \-\-golden golden.jsonl \-\-out prereg/classify.yaml
$EDITOR prereg/classify.yaml   # write hypothesis, stopping_rule, kill_condition
hearth prereg check prereg/classify.yaml \-\-golden golden.jsonl   # fails: uncommitted
git add prereg/classify.yaml && git commit \-m 'prereg: classify'
hearth eval ADAPTER_ID \-\-golden golden.jsonl \-\-prereg prereg/classify.yaml \-\-promote
.fi
.RE
.PP
Try anything without a model (deterministic stub, scratch state):
.RS 4
.nf
HEARTH_BACKEND=echo HEARTH_HOME=/tmp/hearth\-scratch hearth run "hello"
.fi
.RE
